\section{Model and data}{

The gamma process is increasing, with independent increments
\eqn{X_t - X_s \sim \Gamma(a (t - s), b)} (shape \eqn{a(t - s)}, rate
\eqn{b}), so that \eqn{E[X_t] = a t / b} and \eqn{Var(X_t) = a t / b^2}.
The trajectories are independent and have the same law; each one is
observed at its own inspection times. Let
\eqn{(\Delta t_j, \Delta x_j)}, \eqn{j = 1, \dots, m}, be the durations and
degradation increments (\eqn{\Delta x_j > 0}) between two consecutive
inspections of a same trajectory, gathered over all the trajectories,
\eqn{T = \sum_j \Delta t_j}, \eqn{S = \sum_j \Delta x_j},
\eqn{U = \sum_j \Delta t_j \ln \Delta x_j}, the speeds
\eqn{r_j = \Delta x_j / \Delta t_j} and the weights
\eqn{w_j = \Delta t_j / T}.
}

\section{Likelihood}{

The increments being independent, the log-likelihood is
\deqn{\ell(a, b) = \sum_j \big[(a \Delta t_j - 1) \ln \Delta x_j - \ln \Gamma(a \Delta t_j)
  + a \Delta t_j \ln b - b \Delta x_j\big].}
It is a two-parameter exponential family with sufficient statistic
\eqn{(U, S)}.
}

\section{Maximum likelihood estimator}{

\enumerate{
\item The score in \eqn{b} is \eqn{\partial \ell / \partial b = a T / b - S}:
for a fixed \eqn{a}, \eqn{\ell} is maximal at \eqn{\hat b(a) = a T / S}.
\item The profile log-likelihood \eqn{\ell_p(a) = \ell(a, \hat b(a))} has
derivative
\deqn{g(a) = U - \sum_j \Delta t_j\, \psi(a \Delta t_j) + T \ln \frac{a T}{S},}
where \eqn{\psi} is the digamma function.
\item Since \eqn{\psi'(x) > 1/x} for \eqn{x > 0}, \eqn{g} is strictly
decreasing. Moreover \eqn{g(a) \to +\infty} as \eqn{a \to 0} and, by
Jensen's inequality,
\eqn{g(a) \to T \big(\sum_j w_j \ln r_j - \ln \sum_j w_j r_j\big) < 0}
as \eqn{a \to \infty}, unless all the speeds are equal.
\item Hence \eqn{g} has a unique root \eqn{\hat a}, and
\eqn{\hat b = \hat a T / S}. If all the speeds are equal (in particular
if \eqn{m = 1}), the MLE does not exist and the function stops; this has
probability zero when \eqn{m \ge 2}.
}

The root is computed by \code{\link[stats:uniroot]{stats::uniroot()}} in \eqn{\ln a}, starting from the
method of moments: with \eqn{\hat r = S / T} and
\eqn{\hat\sigma^2 = \sum_j (\Delta x_j - \hat r \Delta t_j)^2 / (T - \sum_j \Delta t_j^2 / T)},
\eqn{a_{MM} = \hat r^2 / \hat\sigma^2}. Since \eqn{g} is monotone, the
search converges whatever the starting value.
}

\section{Bias}{

No unbiased version of \eqn{\hat a} and \eqn{\hat b} is available. With few
increments (about ten) both are biased upwards, by about 40 percent; the
bias vanishes as the number of increments grows. Their ratio
\eqn{\hat a / \hat b = S / T} is unbiased.
}

\section{Confidence intervals and estimation failures}{

With \code{ci = TRUE}, the function also returns asymptotic confidence intervals.
Let \eqn{\hat\psi} be the MLE and \eqn{\ell} the log-likelihood. The observed
Fisher information \eqn{J = -\partial^2 \ell / \partial \psi \partial \psi^\top} is
computed at \eqn{\hat\psi} by numerical differentiation (\code{\link[stats:optim]{stats::optimHess()}}),
and the standard errors are the square roots of the diagonal of
\eqn{J^{-1}}. With \eqn{z} the quantile of order \eqn{(1 + level) / 2} of the
standard normal distribution, the interval of a parameter \eqn{\psi_k} is
\itemize{
\item \eqn{\hat\psi_k \pm z\, se_k} for a real parameter (\code{mu} of the Wiener
process, \code{theta} of the variance-gamma process, \code{b} and \code{m} of the Meixner
process);
\item \eqn{\hat\psi_k \exp(\pm z\, se_k / \hat\psi_k)} for a positive parameter: the
interval \eqn{\ln \hat\psi_k \pm z\, se_k / \hat\psi_k} for \eqn{\ln \psi_k}, transformed
back, which always stays positive.
}

These intervals are asymptotic: their coverage is close to \code{level} when the
number of increments (or of trajectories, for the inverse gamma process) is
large. The unbiased estimates \code{s2} (Wiener) and \code{eta_unbiased} (inverse
Gaussian) are given without interval. When \eqn{J} is not positive definite
(estimate close to a boundary of the parameter space), the bounds are \code{NA}.

When the maximum likelihood estimator does not exist for the data (for
instance all the speeds \eqn{\Delta x_j / \Delta t_j} equal, or a maximum at a
boundary of the parameter space), the function does not stop: it gives a
warning with the reason and returns \code{NA} estimates, with the reason in the
attribute \code{"message"}. Invalid data (times not increasing, decreasing levels
for an increasing process, too few observations) still give an error.
}

\examples{
\dontshow{if (requireNamespace("levysim", quietly = TRUE)) withAutoprint(\{ # examplesIf}
# 1. One trajectory inspected every time unit up to 50 (a = 2, b = 3)
t <- seq(0, 50, by = 1)
set.seed(1)
x <- levysim::sim_gamma(t, a = 2, b = 3)
gamma_mle(x)

# The same trajectory given as two vectors: degradation levels, then times
gamma_mle(x[, "X1"], t)

# 2. One trajectory with irregular inspections, more and more frequent
t <- inspection_times("increasing", n = 50, tmax = 50)$time
x <- levysim::sim_gamma(t, a = 2, b = 3)
gamma_mle(x)

# 3. Ten trajectories with the same inspection times
t <- seq(0, 50, by = 5)
x <- levysim::sim_gamma(t, a = 2, b = 3, n_traj = 10)
gamma_mle(x)

# 4. Ten trajectories, each with its own inspection times
plan <- inspection_times("decreasing", n = 5:14, n_traj = 10, tmax = 50)
x <- levysim::sim_gamma(plan, a = 2, b = 3)
gamma_mle(x)

# 5. Estimates with 95 \% confidence intervals
gamma_mle(x, ci = TRUE)

# 6. Your own data: a list of data frames, one per trajectory,
#    with the times in the first column and the levels in the second
liste <- list(data.frame(t = c(0, 10, 20, 30), X = c(0, 6.1, 13.4, 19.8)),
              data.frame(t = c(0, 15, 30), X = c(0, 9.7, 20.5)))
gamma_mle(liste)

# The gamma process only increases: a decrease stops the estimation
try(gamma_mle(c(0, 2, 1.5, 3), c(0, 1, 2, 3)))
\dontshow{\}) # examplesIf}
}
